# Realtime Multiplayer on Bun + Cloudflare Workers (2026)

A single Hono + WebSocket game-room codebase that runs under **Bun** (local dev) and **Cloudflare Workers + Durable Objects** (prod). On Workers the room *is* a Durable Object using the **WebSocket Hibernation API**; on Bun the same room logic runs inside `Bun.serve`. The transport is abstracted behind one small interface so the simulation never imports platform APIs.

## 1. Durable Object WebSocket API (2026)

Two APIs exist; **Hibernation is recommended** for game rooms because it lets the DO sleep while sockets stay open, eliminating idle GB-s charges. ([CF: Use WebSockets](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/), updated 2026-06-19)

- Upgrade flow: Worker validates `Upgrade: websocket`, then `stub.fetch(request)` to the DO. In the DO: `const [client, server] = Object.values(new WebSocketPair())`, then `this.ctx.acceptWebSocket(server)`, then `return new Response(null, { status: 101, webSocket: client })`.
- `acceptWebSocket(ws)` (Hibernation) **vs** `server.accept()` (Standard). Standard pins the DO in memory for the socket's lifetime; Hibernation allows eviction.
- Handlers on the DO class: `webSocketMessage(ws, msg)`, `webSocketClose(ws, code, reason, wasClean)`, `webSocketError(ws, err)`. The runtime re-runs the **constructor** on wake; keep it cheap and rebuild state from `this.ctx.getWebSockets()` + attachments.
- Per-connection state across hibernation: `ws.serializeAttachment(obj)` / `ws.deserializeAttachment()` (max **16,384 bytes**, structured-clone only).
- **Ping/pong:** `this.ctx.setWebSocketAutoResponse(new WebSocketRequestResponsePair('ping', 'pong'))` answers app-level heartbeats **without waking the DO**. ([CF: Build a WebSocket server with WebSocket Hibernation](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/examples/websocket-hibernation-server/); [CF: DurableObjectState / setWebSocketAutoResponse](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/state/))
- **Auto-close:** compat flag `web_socket_auto_reply_to_close` (compat_date **>= 2026-04-07**) makes the runtime auto-reply to Close frames; calling `ws.close()` is then optional.
- **Storage:** SQLite-backed DO storage via `this.ctx.storage.put/get/list/delete` — transactional, strongly consistent, private per DO. ([CF: SQLite-backed DO Storage](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/sqlite-storage-api/))

## 2. Game tick at 15–20 Hz inside a DO

`setInterval`/`setTimeout` **prevent hibernation** and accrue duration charges ([CF: Use WebSockets, "Pricing" note](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/)). The accepted 2026 pattern is the **Alarms API**: each DO holds at most one alarm; the `alarm()` handler does a tick and reschedules itself. ([CF: Alarms](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/), updated 2026-04-21)

- `this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + dt)`, `getAlarm()`, `deleteAlarm()`.
- `alarm()` has at-least-once delivery, exponential backoff from 2 s, up to 6 retries — so always `setAlarm` again before returning to keep the loop alive.
- partyserver's `Party.Server` base class codifies this as `onTick`/`onConnect`/`onMessage`, wrapping hibernation + alarms; PartyKit is the higher-level deployment platform. ([PartyKit](https://github.com/partykit/partykit); [docs](https://docs.partykit.io/))

Minimal fixed-rate room (TypeScript):

```ts
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";
const TICK_MS = 1000 / 20; // 20 Hz

export class GameRoom extends DurableObject {
  constructor(ctx, env) { super(ctx, env); }
  async fetch(req) {
    const [client, server] = Object.values(new WebSocketPair());
    this.ctx.setWebSocketAutoResponse(new WebSocketRequestResponsePair("ping", "pong"));
    this.ctx.acceptWebSocket(server);
    server.serializeAttachment({ id: crypto.randomUUID(), seq: 0 });
    if (!(await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm())) await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + TICK_MS);
    return new Response(null, { status: 101, webSocket: client });
  }
  async webSocketMessage(ws, msg) { /* enqueue input, tagged with client seq */ }
  async alarm() {
    this.step();                 // advance sim, broadcast snapshot
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + TICK_MS); // re-arm
  }
  step() { for (const ws of this.ctx.getWebSockets()) ws.send(this.snapshot()); }
}
```

For an idle lobby you can let the DO hibernate (alarm reschedules only while a match is live); snapshots during a match keep it awake, which is expected.

## 3. Hono → Durable Objects routing

Hono runs as the Worker entrypoint; `export default { fetch: app.fetch }` or `export default app`. Bindings (incl. DO namespaces) are typed via `c.env`. ([Hono: Cloudflare Workers](https://hono.dev/docs/getting-started/cloudflare-workers))

```ts
app.get("/room/:code", (c) => {
  if (c.req.header("Upgrade") !== "websocket") return c.body("Upgrade required", 426);
  const id = c.env.GAME_ROOM.idFromName(c.req.param("code")); // deterministic per room code
  return c.env.GAME_ROOM.get(id).fetch(c.req.raw);
});
```

`idFromName(name)` gives a stable ID per room code (use it for reconnect/affinity); `getByName(name)` is the shortcut shown in CF examples. `wrangler.jsonc` needs a binding **and** a migration:

```jsonc
{
  "name": "fallguys-room", "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-04-07",
  "durable_objects": { "bindings": [{ "name": "GAME_ROOM", "class_name": "GameRoom" }] },
  "migrations": [{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["GameRoom"] }]
}
```

`new_sqlite_classes` declares a new SQLite-backed DO class. ([CF: DO class exports / migrations](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/reference/durable-objects-migrations/); wrangler snippet from the hibernation example)

## 4. Same room, both runtimes

The irreducible difference: **Workers** upgrade via `WebSocketPair` + `Response({webSocket})`; **Bun** upgrades via `server.upgrade(req)` inside `Bun.serve`'s `fetch`, with handlers in a `websocket` object (`open/ws/message/close/drain`). ([Bun: WebSockets](https://bun.sh/docs/api/websockets))

Hide both behind one transport:

```ts
interface RoomTransport {
  onMessage(cb: (peer: Peer, data: Uint8Array) => void): void;
  send(peer: Peer, data: Uint8Array): void;
  broadcast(data: Uint8Array, except?: Peer): void;
  onTick(cb: () => void, hz: number): void; // alarms on CF, setInterval on Bun
}
```

- **Workers impl:** DO class with `webSocketMessage`/`webSocketClose` + `alarm()` tick.
- **Bun impl:** `Bun.serve({ fetch(req, server) { server.upgrade(req, { data: { id } }) }, websocket: { open, message(ws, m){ cb(ws, new Uint8Array(m)) }, close } })`. Hono integrates via `export default { port: 3000, fetch: app.fetch }` and `serveStatic` from `hono/bun`. ([Hono: Bun](https://hono.dev/docs/getting-started/bun)) Hono also ships a `@hono/node-ws`-style `createBunWebSocketHandler` adapter so a Hono route can own the upgrade.

The room simulation (`step()`, snapshot encode, input queue) is pure TS shared by both transports; only the transport file differs. Conditional exports / a runtime check (`typeof Bun`) select the impl, or use two thin entry files (`worker.ts`, `bun.ts`) importing the same `GameRoom` logic.

## 5. Netcode for ~8-player arcade obstacle course

- **Authority:** server-authoritative for physics-critical state (position on moving platforms, fall triggers, collision resolution). Send **inputs** client→server with a monotonic input **sequence number**; server applies them in order and rebroadcasts authoritative snapshots. Client-authoritative-with-validation is cheaper but only safe for cosmetic motion; for a Fall-Guys-style course, server authority prevents wallhacks/teleport.
- **Snapshot interpolation:** client buffers ~2 snapshots and renders ~**100 ms** behind server time, interpolating between the two bracketing snapshots. This hides jitter and reordering without rubber-banding.
- **Input messages (client→server):** `{ seq: u16, t: u32ms, dx: i8, dy: i8, jump: u1, ... }` — a few bytes. Send on change or at input rate; never echo back full state.
- **State snapshot (server→client):** quantize. Per player: `id: u8, x: i16 (cm), y: i16, z: i16, yaw: u16, anim: u4, seq: u16`. 8 players × ~12 B ≈ 96–128 B/payload. Pack one snapshot per tick into a binary frame; CF also recommends **batching** many logical frames into one WebSocket frame to cut context-switch overhead. ([CF: batch messages](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/#batch-messages-to-reduce-overhead))
- **Bandwidth @ 20 Hz:** 128 B × 20 = ~2.6 KB/s outbound per client × 8 = ~20 KB/s room egress — trivially inside DO limits. Inbound is far smaller (inputs). Use `ArrayBuffer`/`Uint8Array` binary frames, not JSON.
- Reconciliation: client sends input seq N; server snapshot includes `lastAckSeq` per player so the client can drop buffered inputs and re-predict.

## 6. Lobby / room codes & reconnect

- **Room code → DO:** `idFromName(roomCode)` (uppercased, 4–6 char base32). Same code routes to the same DO instance across reconnects. Validate the code in the Hono Worker before forwarding (avoids paying for DO spin-up on bad requests).
- **Lobby DO vs Room DO:** a lightweight "matchmaker" DO or just a KV/DO registry maps `code → {status, players, startedAt}`; the actual room is the `GameRoom` DO. Keep `startedAt`/player roster in DO SQLite storage so it survives hibernation.
- **Reconnect:** issue a per-player `sessionId` (UUID) on first join; persist `sessionId → seat` in the DO via `serializeAttachment` (≤16 KB) and `ctx.storage`. On reconnect with the same `sessionId`, restore the seat and replay the last snapshot; don't create a new seat. A short `reconnectGrace` window (e.g. 20–30 s) keeps the seat reserved before treating the player as dropped.
- **Deploy caveat:** deploying new code **disconnects all sockets** (every DO restarts). ([CF: WebSocket disconnection on deploy](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/)) Reconnect logic on the client is therefore mandatory, not optional.

